1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,368
Lantern. An AI care team built on Switch.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:26,084
Every morning at seven, a nurse hands her patients to someone new, and that's when hospitals lose things. Not because people don't care, but because nobody can see who owns what's next. Lantern is a ward built on Switch: four patient rooms with no AI inside them, staff rooms where nurses, a doctor and AI agents work together, and feeds that only our service can write to.

3
00:00:27,184 --> 00:00:36,760
It starts with something small. Patient 101 says she can't wait for breakfast, because Dr. Lee told her it's fine now. But her chart still says nothing by mouth.

4
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:48,636
The intake agent catches that. It doesn't diagnose anything. It quotes her word for word and flags a possible mismatch, and Lantern checks the facts it cites against the record before anyone acts.

5
00:00:49,736 --> 00:00:54,512
Nurse Ana checks with her and confirms. With Wispr Flow she can do that by speaking.

6
00:00:55,612 --> 00:01:06,556
The care-coordinator takes the case to the doctor's room and cites the chart. Every fact gets a receipt. The green tick means it matches the record we logged, not that it's medically right.

7
00:01:07,656 --> 00:01:15,264
Here's the important part. Ana tries to change the order herself, and Lantern refuses. Nurses and agents can't change orders here.

8
00:01:16,364 --> 00:01:26,180
Dr. Lee can. She decides, the chart moves from version three to version four, and the patient gets Dr. Lee's own words on her screen, not an AI's version of them.

9
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:33,496
And the whole ward sees it. Mission control updates from the Switch posts. It's read-only, so nothing can be changed from here.

10
00:01:34,596 --> 00:01:42,804
Anything that needs following up doesn't stay buried in chat. It becomes a Calendar event, linked back to the thread that started it.

11
00:01:43,904 --> 00:01:52,640
And the follow-ups are real. Here is the actual Calendar: Cases seventeen, eighteen and nineteen, each an event linked back to its thread.

12
00:01:53,744 --> 00:02:08,360
And here is the real Jira board. Each case became a task, room-service work sits in To Do, and the finished ones are Done. Nobody typed these in. Lantern's hooks created each task and each event from the thread that started it.

13
00:02:10,384 --> 00:02:20,128
And that decision turns into work somewhere else. A breakfast tray task opens for room service. A nurse can't close it. Only Sam from room service can, once it's delivered.

14
00:02:30,228 --> 00:02:38,004
Then it's seven o'clock. Ana hands over her cases. The coordinator briefs Ben on everything still open, and nothing changes hands until Ben says accept.

15
00:02:39,104 --> 00:02:42,320
When he does, ownership moves everywhere at once.

16
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:51,196
That's Lantern. The agents notice things and carry them. People decide, and people accept. Switch keeps them all in the same room.

